#
# ^ TWO BUGS, both fixed by this commit:
#
# Bug A — the control branch reached `mkfs.xfs` for the first time (the
# mknod fix from run 35049304081 is confirmed working — losetup no longer
# fails), and mkfs.xfs itself refused: the probe images were
# `truncate -s 64M`, below xfsprogs' ~300MB minimum. The step's own control
# guard worked exactly as designed — it caught the broken recipe and voided
# the verdict instead of asserting one. lab-up/lab-seed/lab-verify-refusal/
# the SnapRAID sync never ran (all "skipped" in the job's step list) because
# the AppArmor step failed first.
#
# Bug B — with the AppArmor step failing, lab-destroy (if: always()) ran
# with steps.lab.outputs.id unset, because "Derive this run's lab id" sat
# after the AppArmor step in the job. lab-require-id correctly refused an
# empty HOSERVA_LAB_ID, so teardown died with a second, unrelated failure —
# there was nothing to tear down in this run (lab-up never ran), but the
# workflow could not even establish that cleanly.
#
# Neither bug says anything about AppArmor necessity — that question is
# still open, and still rests on run 35049304081's retracted-as-unsupported
# verdict until the fixed step (512M images, id derived first) runs hosted.
